+            // The PS2 guest address space aliases the same physical word many times over.
+            // ps2ResolveGuestPointer folds KSEG0 (0x20000000), KSEG1 (0x80000000) and the
+            // 0x40000000-0x80000000 window onto the same 32 MB of RDRAM, so a caller that
+            // passes a window spanning them re-reads the same words dozens of times. GT4
+            // searches for the address of one of its own functions and measured
+            // scannedWords=537001983 -- 2 GB -- to find a value that lives in 8,388,608 words
+            // of real RAM, and because the same hit reappeared at every alias it advanced
+            // start past it and found it again, 16 times, without converging.
+            //
+            // Skip any address whose physical word we have already visited. The window is
+            // walked in increasing guest order and aliases fold to low physical offsets, so
+            // this visits every distinct physical word exactly once, in the same order, and
+            // returns the same first match the caller wanted. Physical addresses are monotonic
+            // in the window, so a single high-water mark is sufficient.
+            const bool inRdram = (entryPtr >= rdram) && (entryPtr < (rdram + PS2_RAM_SIZE));
+            if (inRdram)
